1 ADD
  Adds a user to the system authorization file, SYSUAF.DAT. The new
  record starts as a copy of the DEFAULT record, without its flags;
  the qualifiers change what they name.

    ADD username

  A username has at most 12 characters. Without /PASSWORD, the
  password is USER.
2 Qualifiers
/ACCOUNT
    /ACCOUNT=account-name

  The account name.
/ASTLM
    /ASTLM=n

  How many ASTs the user's process may have queued at once.
/BIOLM
    /BIOLM=n

  How many buffered I/O requests the process may have outstanding.
/BYTLM
    /BYTLM=n

  How many bytes of buffer space the process may use for buffered I/O.
/CLI
    /CLI=name

  The command interpreter the user gets at login, DCL.
/DEFPRIVILEGES
    /DEFPRIVILEGES=(privilege[,...])

  The privileges the user's process starts with. Each name sets that
  privilege, NOname clears it; ALL sets every one and NOALL clears
  every one.
/DEVICE
    /DEVICE=device-name[:]

  The user's default device at login.
/DIOLM
    /DIOLM=n

  How many direct I/O requests the process may have outstanding.
/DIRECTORY
    /DIRECTORY=directory-name

  The user's default directory at login, as [NAME].
/ENQLM
    /ENQLM=n

  How many locks the process may hold.
/FILLM
    /FILLM=n

  How many files the process may have open at once.
/OWNER
    /OWNER=owner-name

  The name of the user who owns the account. Quote it to keep blanks
  and lowercase letters.
/PASSWORD
    /PASSWORD=password

  The user's password. It is kept only as a hash.
/PRCLM
    /PRCLM=n

  How many subprocesses the process may have at once.
/PRIORITY
    /PRIORITY=n

  The base priority of the user's process, 0 to 31.
/PRIVILEGES
    /PRIVILEGES=(privilege[,...])

  The privileges the user may enable. Each name sets that privilege,
  NOname clears it; ALL sets every one and NOALL clears every one.
/TQELM
    /TQELM=n

  How many timer queue entries the process may have at once.
/UIC
    /UIC=[group,member]

  The user's user identification code, group and member in octal.
2 Example
    UAF> ADD JOE /PASSWORD=SECRET /UIC=[200,201] /DIRECTORY=[JOE]
    %UAF-I-ADDMSG, user record successfully added
1 EXIT
  Leaves AUTHORIZE. CTRL/Z does the same.

    EXIT
1 HELP
  Describes AUTHORIZE's commands.

    HELP [command]
1 LIST
  Writes the records of the system authorization file to the file
  SYSUAF.LIS in your default directory: one line for each user, or
  each whole record with /FULL.

    LIST [username]

  With no username, or *, it lists every user.
2 Qualifiers
/FULL
    /FULL

  Writes each record whole, as SHOW does, not a line of it.
1 MODIFY
  Changes a user's record in the system authorization file: the
  qualifiers give the new values, and the rest stay as they are.

    MODIFY username
2 Qualifiers
  MODIFY takes the qualifiers ADD does: /ACCOUNT, /ASTLM, /BIOLM,
  /BYTLM, /CLI, /DEFPRIVILEGES, /DEVICE, /DIOLM, /DIRECTORY, /ENQLM,
  /FILLM, /OWNER, /PASSWORD, /PRCLM, /PRIORITY, /PRIVILEGES, /TQELM
  and /UIC. See HELP ADD Qualifiers.
2 Example
    UAF> MODIFY JOE /PRIVILEGES=(TMPMBX,NETMBX,NOOPER)
    %UAF-I-MDFYMSG, user record(s) updated
1 REMOVE
  Takes a user out of the system authorization file.

    REMOVE username
1 SHOW
  Shows a user's record from the system authorization file, or a line
  of it with /BRIEF.

    SHOW username

  SHOW * shows every user.
2 Qualifiers
/BRIEF
    /BRIEF

  Shows a line for the user: the owner, the username, the UIC, the
  account, Normal or All privileges, the priority, and the default
  device and directory.
1 SYSUAF
  AUTHORIZE keeps the users in SYS$SYSTEM:SYSUAF.DAT, or in the file the
  logical name SYSUAF names. Only SYSTEM may change it.

  Run it with MCR AUTHORIZE, or RUN SYS$SYSTEM:AUTHORIZE, and type
  commands at the UAF> prompt; or give one command after MCR AUTHORIZE.

  A record has the username, password, UIC, owner, account, default
  device and directory, command interpreter, priority, privileges and
  quotas. The DEFAULT record holds the values ADD starts from.
